19... Nh4 20. Rh3 { [%emt 0:01:19] [%think 6602] [%out 6904] [%in 2]
[%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1541] The f3 rook is attacked by the knight on h4.
White is up a rook for a pawn, so the priority is to keep the extra material
safe and not trade the rook for the knight. The rook can only go to g3, h3, f2
or f1: - **Rg3** is bad. After Nf5 the knight forks the g3 rook and the e3
bishop. - **Rf2** blocks the bishop's retreat. After Nf5 the bishop cannot drop
back to f2. - **Rh3** is the most active. It hits the h4 knight, which is
protected by the f6 bishop, and later can help attack h7 with the queen. The f4
pawn stays protected by the bishop (and by the queen once the bishop moves). If
Black plays Nf5, the bishop simply retreats to f2. If Black plays Bxc3, White
recaptures bxc3 and the h4 knight is left undefended. } 20... Nf5 21. Qd3 {
